}

const ROOT = '/Users/cstokes/Desktop/Maps'
const ARGS = (typeof args === 'object' && args) ? args : {}
const FABLE_MODEL = ARGS.fableModel || 'opus'
const STANDIN = FABLE_MODEL !== 'fable'
const MAX_LANES = ARGS.maxLanes || 4
const DATE = ARGS.date || '2026-09-28'
const DIR = 'docs/volumes'
const SCRATCH = '/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-cstokes-Desktop-Maps/c0d81c15-f08e-4364-97ca-c6b05aa13811/scratchpad/volumes'
let liveLanes = 0
const laneQueue = []
const acquireLane = () => new Promise((resolve) => { if (liveLanes < MAX_LANES) { liveLanes++; resolve() } else laneQueue.push(resolve) })
const releaseLane = () => { const next = laneQueue.shift(); if (next) next(); else liveLanes-- }
const limited = async (fn) => { await acquireLane(); try { return await fn() } finally { releaseLane() } }
const standInNote = (label) => `

STAND-IN NOTICE: you run on ${FABLE_MODEL} standing in for the Fable seat ${label} (owner, 2026-09-27: "focus all work with opus only"; the owner switched the session to Opus 5.5). Do the full job to Fable's standard. Put under the title of every file you write: > PENDING FABLE REVIEW — written by ${FABLE_MODEL} standing in for the Fable seat ${label}; covered by the whole-design retrovalidation (J84). Add no row to docs/FABLE-REVIEW-QUEUE.md (the owner ruled that the retrovalidation covers the whole, not item by item).`
const seat = (prompt, label, phaseName, schema) => limited(() => agent(STANDIN ? prompt + standInNote(label) : prompt, { label, phase: phaseName, schema, agentType: 'general-purpose', model: FABLE_MODEL }))

const GROUND = `
PROJECT (root ${ROOT}; paths relative to it). docs/ARCHITECTURE.md is the design of PLAT, an original settlement map generator and the settlement map layer for SettlementForge (the owner's definition: docs/DEFINITION.md). PART I, the base (an MFCG/FTG-fused town/city generator), is designed to BUILD depth; the owner's later roadmap stages are designed only to hook or interface depth: PART II (neighbourhood composition, the staged layers II.6, the sewer layer II.7), PART III (one page for the village product), §2.6's stage-3 building hook and plan rung, §4.11's evolution hook, the "# Staged by the owner's roadmap" table, §9.5/§11/§14 for the application and host surfaces. The amendments of 2026-09-27 are being folded in by a bundled pass (docs/AMENDMENTS-2026-09-27c.md items 20 (h) and 32–72 with its Fable review sections, docs/AMENDMENTS-2026-09-27d.md items 73–88 with the owner rulings at its end, docs/AMENDMENTS-2026-09-27e.md item 101 the authoring toolkit); if docs/lane-events.log has a line "amendments-c/closing LANDED" or "amendments-c/validate LANDED", the design already contains them — read the design; otherwise read those items in the amendment files and treat them as part of the design.
NEW OWNER RULES BUNDLED INTO THIS WORK (owner, 2026-09-28 ~17:45: "Can you bundle all of those into the current work?"): docs/AMENDMENTS-2026-09-28g.md — item 102 (sunlight as a soft driver of prosperity: a model-side daylight-share term from terrain, walls, towers and floating islands; the rich prefer the sunlit side; drift over epochs) item 104 with its part (h) (custom content: a custom institution's siting profile resolved from, strongest first, the author's map declaration, its scene archetype, category, tags, the optional library fields and keywords; ACTIVE custom supply chains placed as ONE connected thing — resources at their terrain features, processors stepping inward by the flow, the final seller at the market side, compatible chains clustering into a nameable working quarter, incompatible ones stretching along their road, cross-links to catalogue nodes, services inside their providers, imports and exports through stores at the gate or quay facing the partner) and item 103 with its part (f) (a comprehensive siting rulebook: per-class siting profiles — emits and sensitive-to, needs, clientele, supply, alignment, competition, COVERAGE per catchment — turned into weighted pulls by relation families, SettlementForge tags as default profiles, a sparse signature-pair table, all soft; provable as correlations) — plus the owner's island-shadow expectation (J98; docs/OWNER-INSTRUCTIONS-2026-09-25.md): a floating island's thickness and tapering underside profile shape its shadow, tested under the fixed, cursor and path suns. AND (owner, 2026-09-28 ~18:20: "Integrate these into the current work"), same file: item 105 (the supply web: links between placed members, each consumer bound to its nearest provider, every link pulling BOTH ends; class ORIENTATION source, market or balanced from a goods table; several outputs, one output feeding several chains, hubs; placement order with a bounded relaxation pass for loops; a small supply term in district allocation; SPLIT works-and-shop sites for split classes; SettlementForge's activeChains as input), item 106 (walls that use natural barriers: a barrier closure kind so cliffs, gorges, marsh and the sea close the circuit and only approachable arcs are walled; reaching a nearby barrier with intramural gardens; a wall graded by stretch; the waterfront derived from sea threats; a harbour-mouth chain; never cutting off water access), item 107 (spread and pull in balance: a per-class balance profile, near-enough comfort distances, nearest provider only, same-kind spacing, quarters never assigned — so item 104 (h) (3) is REVISED: a chain sits close at street scale and is named a quarter only past item 72's threshold) and item 108 (SettlementForge's institution ladders: every ladder pair typed COUNT, QUARTER, GUILD, SIZE or REPLACEMENT; a greater tier's members as separate buildings scaled by population; no double counting; ladder steps over time). These are part of the design you work on, with the same authority as the amendment items: the BASE side of every g item (the prosperity driver, the profile columns, the families, the supply web's order and terms, the wall closure and profile, the ladder table, the statistics bars) is applied to the base by the coherence pass from file g, so a volume CITES g's item for the base mechanism and designs only its own part: V05 the island's underside profile, its shadow and its shade strip; V06 shade and siting drift over epochs; V11 the FULL siting rulebook — every class's and every SettlementForge catalogue kind's profile, supply edges, coverage rows and the signature pairs written out as rows — and item 104's rows (the 17 scene-archetype profiles, the category and tag delta tables, the keyword table, the fallback, the custom-chain placement rules); V08 item 104's optional host inputs (the library's definition fields) and the custom chains' arrival through the dossier; V01 the neighbourhood character reading shade and siting; V09 the shade map view and the siting explanations in the interface; V10 the clerk's "why is this here" from the rows that pulled it; and for items 105-108: V11 the goods table and every class's and catalogue kind's orientation, split, balance and ladder step-type rows, plus the wall defensibility and waterfront-threat rows; V01 street-scale closeness, the district supply term and quarter records owning their members; V02 the works-and-shop buildings; V05 an island's rim as a cliff barrier; V06 links re-binding as members open and close, splits over epochs, later wall rings on barriers, coverage gaps reopening, ladder steps over epochs; V07 the palisade's barrier closure; V08 activeChains as a port input, the ladder data and the unnumbered qualifiers; V09 the link view and edits and the wall tool's snapping to barriers; V10 the explanations of links, unwalled sides and a record's members; any other volume where a rule reaches its subject. A volume whose architect already landed gets these parts through its critique and revision; a base change beyond what g states goes to section 10 as usual, citing the g item.
THE OWNER'S INSTRUCTION FOR THIS WORK (2026-09-28): "Can you also comprehensively architect out every aspect now that you have the full system? Might as well get it out the way. And if we need to fix anything [we] just do it down the line." and "So full depth for everything that is not." You write ONE DESIGN VOLUME to BUILD DEPTH for a subject the base carries only at hook depth: after your volume, an implementer holding docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and your volume (and nothing else — the implementer firewall, §17.1) can build the subject without re-deciding anything. "Fix down the line" means: decide every question now (no "TBD", no open choice); where only an experiment can settle a choice, state the chosen mechanism, the confirming measurement and the pre-decided fallback; mistakes are corrected by later amendments, so never stall.
THE STANDARD (the base's own): MECHANISM, NOT ASSERTION — typed data (fields with units, persisted versus derived, identity, and the lifecycle of every state field: create, read, persist, regenerate, undo, migrate), stages and algorithms as steps with inputs, outputs, constants (each with provenance: measured in our captures, read in the reference code readings, our own, or calibrate-later with its default) and failure handling (repair ladders; never a whole-map discard-and-retry, §5), determinism (named random streams, stable addresses, §6), editing (every new state editable by item 101's vocabulary and §7's event kinds; locality; undo), rendering, labels and exports for every new entity (§8, §9, §11), performance budgets per rung (§12), tests and acceptance bars (§13's families; parity metrics against the reference that owns the subject), the build plan (milestones, dependencies, estimates in person-weeks, risk spikes; how it slots into §16), WIRED (owner, 2026-09-28: "every piece of engineering is to be wired to something else" — with the dossier and all the interconnecting pieces): every record and field your volume adds has a named producer and a named consumer, every stage output is consumed, every table is read, every interface has an implementer and a caller, every edit kind has its tool, lifecycle row and undo, every entity kind is rendered, labelled, exported and persisted, every piece plugs into a named base hook, and every dossier fact your stage uses is traced to its dossier field and derivation row (a section 1b WIRING TABLE lists each new piece with what feeds it and what it feeds); and every nontrivial choice recorded as a decision "J-V<volume>.<n>: chose X over Y because Z" (vetoable). THE OWNER'S RULES OUTRANK EVERYTHING: docs/OWNER-INSTRUCTIONS-2026-09-25.md (all dated sections), the amendments files' OWNER RULE items, docs/DEFINITION.md; emulation-first (the reference's behaviour is the default, the owner's rules extend it); the realism tiebreaker (constraint-based, historically faithful; legibility the floor); prosperity is a gradient and a band; population sets size within a tier; everything is editable; the dossier is the sole immutable truth and every gap is derived by our rules (items 20, 20 (h), 22); no hard-coded universals (item 23).
SINGLE SOURCE: state a new fact once, in your volume; for anything the base already states, cite its section — never restate it. Where the base must change for your subject (a new field on a base record, a new stage hook, a contradiction), do NOT edit the base: write a BASE CHANGE REQUEST in your volume's section 10 (the base section's exact heading, the current text quoted once, and the new text in full or the mechanism to add), and design your volume as if it were applied.
CLEAN-ROOM AND STANDALONE: your volume is an implementer input, so the §17.1 rule binds it: no reference code, no reference class or method name, no grammar, palette, word list, constant table or asset copied — describe mechanisms in your own words; cite evidence by file and section only (research/findings/code-*.md are reader-tier: read them to understand, never transfer their identifiers). Read nothing outside ${ROOT} except where your volume's brief allows; never open captures/**/network/bodies/*.js; nothing from partitioned/ except partitioned/README.md (the corpus stays walled off until its own stage; the palette exception, item 69, is already in the design).
VOLUME STRUCTURE (sections, in this order): 0 In plain words (for the owner, who does not code: what this stage does and how it feels to use); 1 Scope, the base hooks consumed (by section) and what is out of scope; 2 Data model (typed interfaces, units, identity, lifecycles); 3 Generation (stages, algorithms, constants with provenance, failure ladders, determinism); 4 Editing (the declarations and edit events, locality, undo, the clerk's view); 5 Rendering, labels, exports; 6 Performance budgets; 7 Tests and acceptance bars (T-V<volume>-<n>), parity metrics; 8 Build plan (milestones, estimates, risks, spikes, where in §16's order); 9 Decisions (J-V rows); 10 Base change requests; 11 Owner questions (only true owner decisions — money, licence, a new capability across the SettlementForge boundary, product order — each with a recommendation); 12 CONFIRMED versus PLAUSIBLE; 13 Fix list (filled by the revision: anything deliberately left for later, per the owner's instruction, with its reason).
WORKING RULES: write your file incrementally — skeleton first with a PROGRESS ledger at the top (one line per section: planned | done), then section by section; KILL-SAFE: the session may be cut at any moment; RESUME RULE: if your output file exists AND docs/lane-events.log has a LANDED line for your lane key, return the structured summary from the file without redoing; CONTINUE RULE: if it exists without one, continue from the first unfinished section. Redirect long tool output to files under ${SCRATCH}/ (make it) and grep them; read whole sections, never samples. Checkpoint at about 300k tokens (write what you have, log a CHECKPOINT line, return the summary with the unfinished sections in open). When complete: echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M') <lane key> LANDED <file>" >> ${ROOT}/docs/lane-events.log  and return the structured summary.`

